      'Off Site in Yoyogi was small enough that loud music was physically impossible — neighbours, thin walls, a PA with opinions. That constraint, more than any manifesto, shaped what came to be called Onkyo: the late-1990s Tokyo practice of extremely quiet, sparse improvisation associated with Sachiko M\u2019s sine-wave sampler, Toshimaru Nakamura\u2019s no-input mixing desk, and a circle including Tetuzi Akiyama, Taku Sugimoto, and Otomo Yoshihide in his quieter turn. The room decided the dynamic range. The musicians decided what to do inside it.',
