      'Western reception, substantially via Jon Abbey\u2019s Erstwhile label, framed Onkyo as the Japanese wing of lowercase — Steve Roden\u2019s term for hushed, small sounds, exemplified by Forms of Paper. The connection is real but partial. Lowercase was largely studio-composed; Onkyo was live improvisation under social constraints, with all the awkwardness that implies: long silences, near-inaudible events, audiences unsure when pieces ended. Erstwhile\u2019s discs, superbly produced, inevitably tidied that awkwardness into aesthetic. The records are essential; they are not the room.',
