      'Begin with a single sound: a low E played on a trombone, recorded and analysed for its overtone content. Now imagine orchestrating that analysis — assigning each audible partial to a different instrument, so that divided strings, horns, and winds collectively resynthesise what one trombone produced. That is the opening move of G\u00e9rard Grisey\u2019s Les Espaces Acoustiques (1974–85), and it contains spectral music\u2019s whole wager: that harmony derived from the acoustic facts of sound will organise large forms better than inherited systems.',
