      'Early power electronics sounded the way it had to: Whitehouse\u2019s Come Organisation LPs and Ramleh\u2019s Broken Flag cassettes were recorded cheaply, mixed brutally, and mastered loud. The murk was partly economics. By the 2000s, with Bird Seed (2003, Susan Lawly) as the hinge, the genre\u2019s production changed. Vocals moved forward and dry. Synth scree acquired definition. The confrontation, rendered in hi-fi, became if anything harder to dismiss — clarity removing the alibi that this was merely noise.',
      'Consumer Electronics\u2019 Dollhouse Songs (2015, Harbinger Sound) is the second wave\u2019s clearest document. Philip Best places his narratives first: vocal takes structured by speech rhythm, electronics arranged around them. The domestic-horror subjects — household interiors as sites of dread — arrive with the intelligibility of radio drama. That intelligibility is the aesthetic gamble. Obscurity lets listeners aestheticise; clarity implicates them.',
      'The production specifics matter. Where first-wave records buried voices in midrange wash, second-wave sessions use close miking, restrained reverb, and careful separation — techniques borrowed from mainstream rock and pop, turned to opposite ends. Haswell\u2019s electronics work on the Dollhouse sessions exemplifies it: precise, layered, every element placed. The menace comes not from overload but from control.',
