      'A contact microphone hears structure, not air. Pressed to ice, wire, or bone, it renders vibration that never becomes airborne sound — the interior of materials. A hydrophone hears pressure in water, where sound travels faster and farther than in air, gathering distant events into dense presence. A parabolic dish hears distance, isolating one bird from a forest. Chris Watson\u2019s Stepping into the Dark (1996, Touch) uses all three, and the album\u2019s variety is transducer variety: each track a study of what one microphone knows.',
