    writerId: 'n-ferreira', publishedAt: '2025-08-07', issueNo: 148, catalogNo: 'CC-148-L', readingMinutes: 10,
    artistIds: ['chris-watson', 'jana-winderen', 'phill-niblock'], releaseIds: ['watson-stepping', 'winderen-energy'], labelIds: ['touch'], genreIds: ['field-recording', 'ambient', 'drone'],
    tags: ['Touch', 'label profile', 'design', 'field recording', 'publishing'],
    pullQuote: 'The sleeve is the first listening instruction. Touch never wastes it.',
    body: [
      'Touch releases look like they sound: spare, exact, unhurried. Since 1981, Jon Wozencroft\u2019s London operation has published field recording, drone, and minimal electronics — Chris Watson\u2019s phonography, Jana Winderen\u2019s hydrophone works, Niblock\u2019s string masses, the long-running Tone and FOLIO series — in sleeves where typography, photography, and empty space do critical work. The design is not decoration. It is the first listening instruction: slow down, this will take time, the details repay.',
      'The catalogue\u2019s coherence comes from refusal. Touch never chased club cycles, never inflated editions, never redesigned for algorithms. Records stay in print or return deliberately; books and prints extend the publishing logic beyond audio. In an industry of campaigns, Touch practices something closer to a press: titles accumulate into a library, each new release recontextualising the back catalogue.',
      'The catalogue holds two overlapping house styles. The field-recording line — Watson, Winderen, Lopez-adjacent releases — treats phonography as composition, with mastering that preserves dynamic extremes. The drone-minimal line — Niblock, organ and string masses — treats duration as content. Both demand systems that reach down and rooms that stay quiet. Touch masters for that listener and trusts the rest to catch up.',
      'Forty-plus years in, the argument holds: slowness sells if the mastering is right and the sleeve tells the truth. Young labels could do worse than study the Touch method — few releases per year, total commitment per release, design as criticism, patience as strategy. The catalogue is the review. It has been positive for decades.',
    ],
