    id: 'e012', slug: 'incus-esp-byg-musician-run-labels', type: 'label-profile',
    title: 'The Artists Alone Decide: Musician-Run Labels from Incus to ESP',
    dek: 'Bailey and Parker pressing their own solos, Stollman recording Ayler in a day, BYG catching the Art Ensemble in Paris. How improvisers built distribution that answered to no one.',
    writerId: 'r-halloway', publishedAt: '2024-09-05', issueNo: 125, catalogNo: 'CC-125-L', readingMinutes: 11,
    artistIds: ['derek-bailey', 'evan-parker', 'albert-ayler', 'art-ensemble'], releaseIds: ['parker-solos', 'ayler-spiritual', 'ae-jackson'], labelIds: ['incus', 'esp-disk', 'byg-actuel'], genreIds: ['free-improv'],
    tags: ['Incus', 'ESP-Disk', 'BYG', 'label profile', 'DIY', 'free jazz'],
    pullQuote: 'No permission sought, none required. The motto was the business plan.',
    body: [
      'Incus Records, founded in 1970 by Derek Bailey, Evan Parker, and Tony Oxley, existed because no one else would document British free improvisation properly. The catalogue\u2019s core — Bailey\u2019s solos, Parker\u2019s Saxophone Solos (1976) — remains the music\u2019s reference shelf. Musician-run meant musician-paced: records appeared when players judged them ready, in sleeves that wasted nothing, sold through gigs and mail order. The economics were precarious; the archive is priceless.',
      'Bernard Stollman\u2019s ESP-Disk worked the complementary model: an enthusiast with a motto (\u201cthe artists alone decide what you will hear\u201d) and a fast session clock. Albert Ayler\u2019s Spiritual Unity (1965) was recorded in a day and released the same year — speed as aesthetic, cheapness as honesty. The ESP catalogue\u2019s roughness is inseparable from its daring: many sessions, minimal intervention, history captured before it could be second-guessed.',
      'BYG Actuel caught the third configuration: Americans in Paris. The Art Ensemble of Chicago\u2019s 1969 residency produced A Jackson in Your House and companion sessions in long, underfunded takes. Funds were short, tape ran long, results were historic. The Actuel festival context — free music staged as countercultural event — gave the recordings an occasion their intensity still carries.',
      'Three models, one lesson. Improvised music, which happens once and vanishes, depends on people willing to record it without waiting for permission — players, enthusiasts, exiles with tape machines. Every free-improv disc this publication reviews descends from these three decisions to press record. The artists decided. We are still hearing what they heard.',
    ],
    factBox: ['Incus founded 1970 (Bailey, Parker, Oxley)', 'ESP-Disk founded 1964; motto as business plan', 'BYG Actuel: Paris sessions, 1969 residencies', 'All catalogues substantially in print via reissues'],
    listenTo: ['Evan Parker — Saxophone Solos', 'Albert Ayler — Spiritual Unity', 'Art Ensemble of Chicago — A Jackson in Your House'],
